<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Tbilisi</h1><p class="st-meta">736 CE – 1122 CE (approx.) · capital Tbilisi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim emirate centred on Tbilisi that ruled the heart of eastern Georgia for nearly four centuries until David the Builder took the city in 1122.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arab armies first took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in the 640s, and around 736 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan ibn Muhammad</a> made it the seat of an emir governing Georgian lands. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> turned the strategic city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Mtkvari</a> into a largely self-governing state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> was a prosperous trading city of Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, with a mint, baths and markets. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, notably the Jaffarid family, often defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishaq-ibn-ismail">Ishaq ibn Ismail's</a> rebellion ended in 853 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugha-the-elder">Bugha the Elder</a> burned the city. Later the emir shared power with a council of leading citizens.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by Georgian pressure, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> fell under Seljuk control in the 1080s. In 1122 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-didgori">Battle of Didgori</a> and made it his capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-tbilisi.jpg" alt="Emirate of Tbilisi" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Constantine Plakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_1060_map_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>736 CE – 1122 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tbilisi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ishaq ibn Ismail</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic; Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tbilisi Emirate, Jaffarid Emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=929&amp;focus=emirate-of-tbilisi" aria-label="Emirate of Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Tbilisi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6091 947L6134 1224M6091 947L6134 1224M6091 947L6481 1127M6091 947L6036 930"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6134 1224h0M6481 1127h0M6036 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tbilisi. <a href="/atlas?year=929&amp;focus=emirate-of-tbilisi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

